{'review': '{\n  "review": {\n    "overall_status": "NEEDS_FIXES",\n    "quality_score": 5.0,\n    "summary": {\n      "critical": 0,\n      "high": 2,\n      "medium": 2,\n      "low": 1,\n      "passed_checks": [\n        "Type annotations used correctly",\n        "No hardcoded secrets",\n        "Authorization header handled conditionally"\n      ]\n    },\n    "findings": [\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-001",\n        "severity": "high",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing timeout for API requests",\n        "file": "src/utils/api.ts",\n        "line_range": [30, 34],\n        "description": "The fetch request lacks a timeout mechanism, which can cause hanging requests and consume client resources indefinitely during network issues or slow server responses.",\n        "impact": "Users may experience unresponsive UI; excessive resource consumption (memory, connections) on the client; potential denial-of-service or slow server scenarios.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Implement timeout using AbortController with a reasonable duration (e.g., 10 seconds). Clear timeout on success/error and throw a specific timeout error.",\n        "code_before": "  const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}${endpoint}`, config);\\n\\n  if (!response.ok) {\\n    const errorData = await response.json().catch(() => ({ message: response.statusText }));\\n    throw new Error(errorData.message || \'Something went wrong\');\\n  }",\n        "code_after": "  const controller = new AbortController();\\n  const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10000); // 10s timeout\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}${endpoint}`, { ...config, signal: controller.signal });\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId);\\n    \\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      const errorData = await response.json().catch(() => ({ message: response.statusText }));\\n      throw new Error(errorData.message || \'Something went wrong\');\\n    }\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId);\\n    if (error.name === \'AbortError\') {\\n      throw new Error(\'Request timeout\');\\n    }\\n    throw error; // Re-throw network errors and other fetch errors\\n  }\\n\\n  // Handle cases where response might be empty (e.g., DELETE requests)\\n  if (response.status === 204) {\\n    return {} as T; // Return an empty object for no content responses\\n  }\\n\\n  return response.json();",\n        "references": [\n          "CWE-404: Improper Resource Shutdown or Release",\n          "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-002",\n        "severity": "high",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Incorrect handling of HTTP 204 No Content responses",\n        "file": "src/utils/api.ts",\n        "line_range": [39, 42],\n        "description": "The function returns an empty object (`{} as T`) for 204 responses, which violates the generic type contract when the caller expects a non-empty structure (e.g., `todosApi.delete` expects `{ message: string }`).",\n        "impact": "Runtime errors when callers access properties on the returned value (e.g., `undefined` property access), leading to broken UI and poor user experience.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Return `null` for 204 responses and update callers to handle null, or change the return type to `T | null` and document that 204 returns null.",\n        "code_before": "  // Handle cases where response might be empty (e.g., DELETE requests)\\n  if (response.status === 204) {\\n    return {} as T; // Return an empty object for no content responses\\n  }\\n\\n  return response.json();",\n        "code_after": "  // Handle cases where response might be empty (e.g., DELETE requests)\\n  if (response.status === 204) {\\n    return null as unknown as T; // Return null for no content responses\\n  }\\n\\n  return response.json();",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-003",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing validation of endpoint parameter leading to potential SSRF",\n        "file": "src/utils/api.ts",\n        "line_range": [7, 7],\n        "description": "The `endpoint` parameter is directly concatenated to `API_BASE_URL` without validation, allowing potential SSRF if the endpoint contains a full URL (e.g., \'http://evil.com\').",\n        "impact": "Attackers could trick the application into making requests to arbitrary domains, potentially leaking sensitive data (like auth tokens) if the attacker\'s domain is configured to accept CORS credentials.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Validate that the endpoint is a relative path (starts with \'/\' and does not contain \'://\' or \'..\') before use. Reject invalid endpoints with a clear error.",\n        "code_before": "export const apiRequest = async <T>(endpoint: string, options: RequestOptions = {}): Promise<T> => {\\n  const { isAuthRequired = true, headers, ...restOptions } = options;\\n  const token = localStorage.getItem(\'accessToken\'); // Directly access token for utility function\\n\\n  const config: RequestInit = {\\n    ...restOptions,\\n    headers: {\\n      \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\',\\n      ...headers,\\n    },\\n  };",\n        "code_after": "export const apiRequest = async <T>(endpoint: string, options: RequestOptions = {}): Promise<T> => {\\n  // Validate endpoint to prevent SSRF\\n  if (!endpoint.startsWith(\'/\') || endpoint.includes(\'://\') || endpoint.includes(\'../\')) {\\n    throw new Error(\'Invalid endpoint: must be a relative path starting with /\')\\n  }\\n\\n  const { isAuthRequired = true, headers, ...restOptions } = options;\\n  const token = localStorage.getItem(\'accessToken\'); // Directly access token for utility function\\n\\n  const config: RequestInit = {\\n    ...restOptions,\\n    headers: {\\n      \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\',\\n      ...headers,\\n    },\\n  };",\n        "references": [\n          "CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)",\n          "https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/SSRF/"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-004",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing error handling for network failures",\n        "file": "src/utils/api.ts",\n        "line_range": [32, 32],\n        "description": "Network errors (e.g., DNS failure, connection refused) thrown by `fetch` are not caught, causing the promise to reject with a raw `TypeError` that callers may not handle appropriately.",\n        "impact": "Inconsistent error handling; callers receive unhelpful `TypeError: Failed to fetch` instead of domain-specific errors, complicating error UI and retry logic.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Wrap the `fetch` call in a try-catch block to distinguish network errors from HTTP errors and rethrow as a structured error.",\n        "code_before": "  const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}${endpoint}`, config);\\n\\n  if (!response.ok) {\\n    const errorData = await response.json().catch(() => ({ message: response.statusText }));\\n    throw new Error(errorData.message || \'Something went wrong\');\\n  }",\n        "code_after": "  try {\\n    const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}${endpoint}`, config);\\n    \\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      const errorData = await response.json().catch(() => ({ message: response.statusText }));\\n      throw new Error(errorData.message || \'Something went wrong\');\\n    }\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    if (error.name === \'TypeError\' && error.message.includes(\'Failed to fetch\')) {\\n      throw new Error(\'Network error: Unable to reach the server\');\\n    }\\n    throw error; // Re-throw other errors (e.g., timeout, JSON parse)\\n  }",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-005",\n        "severity": "low",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Unhandled non-JSON success responses",\n        "file": "src/utils/api.ts",\n        "line_range": [44, 44],\n        "description": "The function assumes all successful responses (status 2xx) are JSON-parsable. If the API returns non-JSON (e.g., plain text, HTML), `response.json()` will throw a `SyntaxError` that is not caught.",\n        "impact": "Application crashes with `SyntaxError` when API unexpectedly returns non-JSON data, leading to poor user experience and difficulty debugging.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Check the `Content-Type` header before parsing JSON. For non-JSON responses, return the raw text or throw a meaningful error.",\n        "code_before": "  return response.json();",\n        "code_after": "  const contentType = response.headers.get(\'content-type\') || \'\';\\n  if (contentType.includes(\'application/json\')) {\\n    return response.json();\\n  }\\n  // For non-JSON responses (e.g., plain text), return text\\n  return response.text() as unknown as T;",\n        "references": [\n          "CWE-20: Improper Input Validation",\n          "https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Improper_Input_Validation"\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    "positive_observations": [\n      "TypeScript generics used correctly for type-safe API responses",\n      "Authorization header is conditionally added only when required",\n      "Error responses are parsed safely with fallback to statusText",\n      "Common API functions (authApi, todosApi) demonstrate proper usage of the utility",\n      "No hardcoded secrets or credentials in the code"\n    ]\n  }\n}'}